# Line endings are declared, not inferred from each machine's autocrlf setting.
#
# The .bat file MUST be CRLF: cmd.exe mis-parses LF-only batch files. Everything
# else is LF, so a Linux/CI checkout behaves the same as a Windows one.
#
# WHAT THIS DOES NOT GUARANTEE — worth knowing before chasing it again:
#
#   `text=auto` marks the file as text and normalizes it to LF IN THE REPOSITORY.
#   On checkout, a local `core.autocrlf=true` still converts the WORKING COPY to
#   CRLF. Verified on this machine: `git config --get core.autocrlf` returns true,
#   `git check-attr text eol -- .gitignore` reports `text: auto, eol: lf`, and yet
#   the working copy comes back CRLF.
#
#   So the repo and every clone are LF, but a CRLF working copy here is expected
#   rather than a mistake. `git status` will not show it, because the index already
#   holds LF. Use `eol=lf` (not `text=auto`) on a path when the WORKING COPY must be
#   LF too; the .bat/.cmd lines below do exactly that, in the other direction.
#
#   A file added by an editor that writes CRLF (PowerShell's `Add-Content` does)
#   shows up as "CRLF will be replaced by LF the next time Git touches it" — that
#   warning is about the working copy, not about what gets committed.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Force the working copy too, not just the repository: cmd.exe needs real CRLF.
*.bat  text eol=crlf
*.cmd  text eol=crlf

*.png  binary
*.tgz  binary
*.db   binary
